 ## 2026-09-02
 
+### 2122 — A byte bound measured on Go's escaped encoding is a bound nobody declared
+
+- ROOT CAUSE: `validateSessionLaunchPlan` and `internal/config`'s `validateExtensions` both measured a declared 65,536-byte bound with `len(json.Marshal(value))`. `encoding/json` rewrites each of the single bytes `<`, `>` and `&` into a six-character escape, and U+2028/U+2029 from three canonical bytes into six, so the bound in force was up to 6x tighter than the pinned one and was a property of `encoding/json` rather than of the specification. It failed closed — nothing oversized was admitted — but a CONFORMING document was refused, and the third site, `validateExtensionsObject`, already measured canonical bytes. One repository carried both the defect and its fix.
+- FINDING: `SetEscapeHTML(false)` alone does NOT fix it. `encoding/json` escapes U+2028 and U+2029 regardless of that setting, so only the RFC 8785 transform makes the measurement independent of the encoder. Probed directly: `["<"]` is 5 canonical bytes and 10 marshalled; `[U+2028]` is 7 canonical and 10 marshalled.
+- FIX: `internal/canonicaljson/declared_byte_bounds.go` — `CanonicalByteLength` (encode with `SetEscapeHTML(false)`, then `Canonicalize`) is the one measurement, and `canonicalByteBound(name, value, maximum)` is the one refusal gate. `closed_shapes.go:284` and `closed_shapes.go:1794` call the gate; `internal/config/validation.go:763` calls the measurement. `internal/config` now imports `internal/canonicaljson`; no cycle, canonicaljson imports only `catalog` and `scalar`.
+- FINDING: `canonicalByteBound`'s signature (`name string` + `maximum int`) matches the derived bound-helper shape in `declared_bounds_test.go`, so both byte bounds became first-class derived obligations automatically. Two `boundaryConstraintCase` entries now claim them, and changing either literal reddens the derivation gate as well as the new tests. Sharing the gate was chosen for deduplication; wiring into the derived-obligation inventory was a free consequence of the existing scanner, not a hand-registered one.
+- DECISION: folding both call sites onto `canonicalByteBound` instead of leaving `if err != nil { ... }` inline at each. The inline shape measured 64 uncovered statements in `internal/canonicaljson` against a baseline of 63 (97.143% vs 97.179%), because each call site carried its own unreachable measure-error guard. The shared gate carries one, and the final tree is 63 uncovered / 97.185% — coverage slightly above baseline rather than below it.
+- EVIDENCE: 8 mutants, 8 killed. M1 (`CanonicalByteLength` returns the marshalled length) reddens all four canonicaljson tests and both config tests. M2-M5 narrow and widen each canonicaljson bound by one; M6-M7 do the same to `maxConfigExtensionBytes`; M8 restores the escaped measurement at the config site alone. Narrow/widen mutants matter here because a delete-only mutant proves the gate exists and says nothing about where it sits. Transcript: `BUG-260902-3v8ck5_mutation-sweep.log`.
+- NOTE: the tests are built from characters whose two encodings differ, plus one encoding-neutral ASCII control row that shares the same fixture machinery. Every row asserts the accepted at-limit document is OVER the bound when measured the escaped way — that assertion, not the acceptance, is what kills the mutant. Fixture sizes are verified through `Canonicalize`, not through the helper under test, and the limit is a local SPEC literal, never `maxConfigExtensionBytes`.
+- SCOPE: `internal/canonicaljson/declared_byte_bounds.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/closed_shapes.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/canonical_byte_bound_measurement_test.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/boundary_constraints_test.go`, `internal/canonicaljson/refusal_guards_test.go`, `internal/config/validation.go`, `internal/config/extension_canonical_byte_bound_test.go`
